<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;"><b>Theme:</b> Our Historical Sites</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions in personal stories. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from audio stories about Indigenous traditions in Panama (e.g., "What materials are used to make the Emberá clothing?" "What instruments are played during Guna ceremonies?" "What foods are prepared for traditional Ngäbe-Buglé festivals?"). Can interpret cultural descriptions and provide feedback (e.g., "I think we can add a description of the traditional dance that the Emberá people perform at their festivals. It's a big part of their celebrations."). Can interpret peer feedback on a project about Indigenous cultures by summarizing key suggestions and identifying areas for improvement (e.g., "My classmate liked the cultural elements in our project but suggested adding more pictures of the Guna people wearing traditional clothing to better showcase their culture.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images of famous Panamanian historical sites (e.g., Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo ruins). The teacher briefly describes each site, one by one, using simple past and present simple, while students listen and point to the corresponding image. For example: "This is a very famous landmark, the Panama Canal. It connects two oceans and was built many years ago. Many visitors come to explore it."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: 'historical sites,' 'Panama Canal,' 'Casco Viejo,' 'landmarks,' 'architecture,' 'heritage,' 'monument,' 'visitor,' 'preserve,' 'explore,' 'attract,' 'inform.' Emphasis is placed on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "cul<b>ture</b>," "his<b>to</b>rical," "in<b>form</b>") and distinguishing minimal pairs (e.g., /ɪ/ in 'sit' from 'visitor' and /iː/ in 'seat' related to 'explore' or 'meet'). The teacher repeats each word clearly, and students listen and repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled vocabulary using concept checking questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a historical site an old building or a new building?" (Expected answer: "An old building.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do people go to 'explore' a place to just look or to discover new things?" (Expected answer: "To discover new things.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'preserve' mean? To keep something safe or to destroy it?" (Expected answer: "To keep something safe.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to an audio recording (MP3 file) of a local tour guide describing Casco Viejo, focusing on its history, architecture, and what visitors can see and do there. The audio is approximately 1.5 - 2 minutes long. Genre: Informative audio description. Register: Semi-formal. Format: Short narrative podcast segment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Participate in conversations about travel plans and historical knowledge, providing relevant descriptions and responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher asks, "What is the main topic of this audio? Which historical site is the guide describing?" Students listen for the general idea.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detail Questions (Second Listen):</b> The teacher provides a few True/False statements or multiple-choice questions on a worksheet. Students listen again and select the correct answers based on specific details mentioned in the audio. Example: "Casco Viejo is a modern city." (False) "The architecture in Casco Viejo is very old." (True). "Visitors can find museums there." (True).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of detailed descriptions. Grammar: Present simple and simple past for describing places and their importance (e.g., "Casco Viejo is very attractive to tourists because it was once a vibrant colonial village."), possessive forms with 's (e.g., "Panama's historical sites"). Vocabulary: historical sites, Casco Viejo, architecture, heritage, monument, destination, visitor, explore, preserve, describe, inform.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Listening:</b> Students receive a handout with a partial transcript of the audio, containing gaps for specific vocabulary words or verb forms (present simple/past simple). The teacher plays the audio for a third time. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing words/phrases they hear. (e.g., "Casco Viejo ______ (is) a famous historical ______ (destination). It ______ (was) a vibrant colonial village and its beautiful ______ (architecture) still ______ (attracts) many ______ (visitors) today.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Identifying Specific Details:</b> The teacher prepares a list of sentences related to the audio, some correct and some containing slight factual errors. The teacher reads each sentence aloud. Students listen and indicate (e.g., by raising a green card for 'correct' and a red card for 'incorrect' or writing 'C'/'I') if the sentence accurately reflects what they heard in the audio. This focuses purely on receptive listening without requiring oral production beyond a simple signal. Example: "The buildings in Casco Viejo are all very new." (Incorrect) "Casco Viejo's heritage is preserved." (Correct).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the previous gap-fill and detail identification activities. They are instructed to verbally explain *what they heard* to justify their answers. For instance, "For number 3, I heard the guide say 'its architecture still attracts many visitors,' so I wrote 'attracts'." This encourages interactive listening and interpretation of peer feedback, addressing the learning outcome.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Listening and Information Gathering.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a new, short audio recording (approx. 1.5 min) describing a different Panamanian historical site (e.g., the Panama Viejo ruins or the San Lorenzo Fort). This audio may feature slightly different accents or a personal narrative tone. While listening, each group is tasked with collaboratively completing a "Historical Site Fact File" worksheet. The worksheet prompts students to fill in details *heard* from the audio, such as: "Site Name," "Location," "When was it important (past simple)?", "Why is it a monument today (present simple)?", "What makes it a tourist destination?", "What can visitors learn there?". Students must listen actively and consult with their group members to ensure accuracy and completeness of the information they extract from the audio. They practice interpreting peer feedback ("Did you hear 'colony' or 'city'?" "I heard 'colony' because she said 'it was once a Spanish colony.'").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly applies listening skills to gather information about Panamanian historical sites for tourism, using target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion. Students demonstrate their ability to identify details from audio stories and interpret peer feedback effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and individual comprehension check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a final, short (approx. 1 minute) audio clip—a brief personal story from a Panamanian about a family visit to a local historical site. Students individually answer 3-4 specific detail comprehension questions (multiple choice or short answer) on an exit ticket, based *only* on this audio. Example questions: "Who did the speaker visit the site with?" "What did they do there?" "What did the speaker think about the site?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of key details from audio recordings (e.g., facts, feelings, plans).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of comprehension in written responses.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in collaborative listening activities and effective interpretation of peer feedback.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new piece of information you learned about historical sites in Panama today from listening?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to today's audio descriptions?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did you use today to help you understand the audio recordings better (e.g., listening for keywords, taking notes)?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident are you now in identifying key information when listening to descriptions of historical sites in English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the lesson worked well (e.g., student engagement with the audio, clarity of instructions, effectiveness of pair work)? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for future listening lessons (e.g., pre-teaching more vocabulary, providing visual aids during listening, slower audio speeds)? Did students demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions at an A2.4 level? Was the focus on listening adequately maintained?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The vocabulary words and understanding of Panamanian historical sites introduced through listening today will be reinforced in the next lesson, which will focus on reading comprehension using a written text about similar historical sites and their importance.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>